 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "ABERDEEN (Obar Dheathain). The Granite City. 'Obar Dheathain' means 'mouth of the Don'. The big grey buildings are built of local granite that sparkles in the sun, thanks to the mica. It sparkles for about an hour a year.",
   "x": 2.0,
   "y": 21.0
  },
  {
   "text": "MARISCHAL COLLEGE. The second largest granite building in the world, finished in 1906 with a spiky pinnacled front. It's now the council offices, so you can pay your parking fine under a Gothic ceiling.",
   "x": 28.0,
   "y": 16.0
  },
  {
   "text": "ABERDEEN HARBOUR. Founded before 1136, it's Scotland's oldest business. Ferries for Orkney and Shetland sail from here. Look out for the dolphins at Torry Battery, and sniff out the fish market.",
   "x": 52.0,
   "y": 33.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE MARITIME MUSEUM. A towering model of an offshore oil rig, a model of a clipper ship called the Thermopylae and a very sensible hat.",
   "x": 33.0,
   "y": 33.0
  },
  {
   "text": "UNION STREET. A mile of granite built on a bridge over a ravine, so the shops on the street don't know that underneath them there's a valley. Hope the foundations don't, either.",
   "x": 38.0,
   "y": 21.0
  },
  {
   "text": "FOOTDEE (Fittie). A tiny seaside village of fishermen's cottages arranged in squares, with their backs to the North Sea gales. They've been rebuilt many times, and are still lovely.",
   "x": 44.0,
   "y": 38.0
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Lord Byron, poet, spent his boyhood in Aberdeen from 1790 to 1798 and went to the Grammar School.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": 0.475,
   "plaque": "aberdeen_byron",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Lord Byron"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "aberdeen_mercat_cross",
   "title": "Mercat Cross",
   "text": "Aberdeen's mercat cross, the market cross where royal proclamations were read out, was built in the Castlegate in 1686. It is carved with portrait medallions of the Stuart monarchs and topped with a unicorn, Scotland's national animal. The unicorn has never once been caught grazing."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "aberdeen_kings_college",
   "title": "King's College",
   "text": "Bishop William Elphinstone founded King's College in Old Aberdeen in 1495, and its chapel tower still wears a stone crown. With Marischal College as well, Aberdeen had two universities, as many as the whole of England at the time. Students have been late for lectures here for over five centuries."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "aberdeen_brig_o_balgownie",
   "title": "Brig o' Balgownie",
   "text": "The Brig o' Balgownie, a single Gothic arch over the River Don, was finished in the early 1300s. An old rhyme said it would fall under 'a wife's ae son' on 'a mare's ae foal', ae meaning one. Lord Byron, an only son, leaned over it anyway, with what he called childish delight. The bridge is still standing."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "aberdeen_finnan_haddie",
   "title": "Finnan Haddie",
   "text": "Finnan haddie is haddock, split, salted and cold-smoked, named after Findon, a fishing village just south of Aberdeen. Poach it in milk and you have breakfast. Add potato and onion and you are halfway to Cullen skink. Either way, the kitchen will smell of it until Thursday."
  }
 ],
